sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: ADR 0243/0244 task, DECISIONS R1-R4, agent-observer-swamp-acts, single-swamp-node. docs/reference/node-provisioning.md#converge-the-orbit-footprint.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: node:converge
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh
  CLI->>GW: node:converge 12<br/>--force
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit node:converge 12 --force`
  %%> - syntax: `<node> [--force] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`
  %%> Input:
  %%> - the Node, by id or exact name
  %%> - `--force` re-applies every part, not only the parts whose digest changed
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - re-applies Orbit's own files on Node 12: the Node agent, the private DNS client policy, and the files of its active roles
  %%> - never changes an Instance, a Process, a Schedule or a role assignment, so it is safe on a Node that owns Instances
  %%> Who can call it:
  %%> - the `orbit` CLI, the MCP tool, and the fleet rollout send the same request
  Note over GW: Render the footprint<br/>task #5132 · digest per part
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12
  %%> - Node 12 is an active, managed Linux Node
  %%> - no other converge or Node agent rekey runs for Node 12; the Gateway holds one lock per Node
  %%> Builds:
  %%> - the pinned `orbit-agent` release and its SHA-256
  %%> - the Node agent configuration, Orbit root certificate and unit
  %%> - the private DNS client policy, when Node 12 is a peer with roles
  %%> - the files of each active role, through the shared role steps
  %%> - one digest per part, from the Gateway's own code and pins; no SSH
  %%> Result:
  %%> - task #5132 recorded with the full intent and the parts to apply
  %%> - unchanged parts are left out, unless `--force`
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-node-converge
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)
  %%> Inputs:
  %%> - the parts to apply, with their files and digests
  %%> - the agent's verifier hash, to check the existing key; never the key
  %%> - Node 12's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role
  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5132
  %%> What you get:
  %%> - the task number, at once
  %%> Next:
  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`
  Note over SW: Read the scripts<br/>@nckrtl/node-scripts read
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - reads the shipped `agent.py` script and the agent part of the intent on 209
  %%> - a part the Gateway could not render makes the next step refuse with its code
  SW->>N: Install the Node agent<br/>agent.py install · sha256
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - compares the installed binary with the pin; a match skips the rest of this step
  %%> - otherwise downloads the release, checks its SHA-256, and swaps it in, keeping `orbit-agent.previous`
  %%> - other running services of the old binary, such as `plausible`, restart on the new one
  %%> How:
  %%> - a shipped script through `@swamp/ssh` from 209, with sudo
  %%> - no `orbit-agent command …`; the agent only observes and runs no actions
  SW->>N: Configure the Node agent<br/>agent.py configure · same key
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - writes `config.toml`, `ca.pem` and the `orbit-agent.service` unit in `/etc/orbit/agent`
  %%> - restarts the agent only when the binary or one of these files changed
  %%> Keeps the key:
  %%> - checks that `/etc/orbit/agent/secret` matches the recorded verifier; it never writes a new key
  %%> - a missing or mismatched key fails this step; `orbit:node-agent-rekey` issues a new one
  %%> - removes the world bits from `.env` in each Instance checkout under the apps root
  %%> How:
  %%> - the same shipped script through `@swamp/ssh` from 209, with sudo
  opt Node 12 is a DNS peer
    SW->>N: Apply private DNS policy<br/>resolver for .orbit domains
    %%> When:
    %%> - Node 12 is an active managed peer with roles; not the `vpn` Node and not the Gateway's Node
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - sends the private `.orbit` domains to the Orbit DNS server, with the split DNS policy
    %%> - no WireGuard tunnel restart
  end
  opt Node 12 has active roles
    SW->>N: Re-apply role files<br/>node-converge-role-files
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - runs one child workflow with the fleet recipe's agent-free role steps, only for parts whose digest changed
    %%> Per role:
    %%> - a Caddy role or Caddy sites: Caddy package floor, then the Caddyfile; validates it first and reloads Caddy gracefully
    %%> - `app-dev`, `app-prod`: the managed PHP-FPM module; a graceful reload of valid running masters
    %%> - `app-dev`: the `/etc/tmpfiles.d/orbit.conf` rule for `/tmp/orbit-*` and `/dev/shm/orbit-*`; restarts nothing
    %%> - `vpn`: the private DNS listener, records and catalog; restarts dnsmasq or the listener only for a change, and restores the last good copy when the new one fails its check
    %%> - Route residue: removes the Caddy sites, PHP pools, certificates and LAN rules of removed Routes, best effort
    %%> Checks:
    %%> - each role step checks its own part: Caddy validates the Caddyfile, DNS answers, PHP-FPM masters reload
    %%> Not included:
    %%> - adding or removing a role, and Metrics exporters; those have their own commands
  end
  SW->>N: Check the footprint<br/>agent.py check
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - checks that `orbit-agent` is active and runs the pinned binary
  %%> - reads the agent's files back and compares them with their content
  %%> Why:
  %%> - a part only counts as applied when the Node shows it
  SW->>SW: Report the result<br/>@nckrtl/node-scripts
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - runs on 209 after every other step, even after a failure
  %%> - lists each part as applied, unchanged or skipped, from the steps that succeeded
  %%> Returns:
  %%> - the result the Gateway reads; it holds no secret
  SW->>SW: Prove the footprint<br/>assert · no error_code
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - fails the run when a part did not apply, so the task fails; the result above stays readable
  alt every part is applied
    N-->>SW: digest per part
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the check passed for every part
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
    %%> How the Gateway learns it:
    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway
    Note over GW: Record footprint digests<br/>Node 12 · task succeeded
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the digest of each applied part, and Node 12's footprint digest
    %%> - the fleet rollout sees Node 12 as up to date
    %%> - task #5132 succeeds
  else a part fails
    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or no match
    %%> When:
    %%> - a download doesn't match its pin, the Node agent's key doesn't match, Caddy refuses the Caddyfile, or a check fails
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed part
    %%> The Gateway reads:
    %%> - which part failed and its error code, and which parts finished before it
    Note over GW: Record finished parts<br/>task failed · rerun retries rest
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the digests of the finished parts are recorded; the failed part keeps its old digest
    %%> - when the role files step fails, every role part keeps its old digest
    %%> - task #5132 fails
    %%> On the Node:
    %%> - finished parts stay; there is no rollback, except the DNS listener's restore of its last good copy
    %%> - the running Node agent keeps its key, so it keeps reporting
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - run the same command again after fixing the cause; it applies only the parts that are still different
  end
  loop every 2 s until terminal
    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5132
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended
    GW-->>CLI: status + result
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended
  end
